  data:  # 9e33
    kind: invariant  # 9e33
    title: "Work done once and shared (a memoised function, a module or session fixture, a subprocess) leaves most of its dependents unpaired: selecting test by test leaves out failing tests"  # 9e33
    status: violated  # 9e33
    priority: 2  # 9e33
    parent:  # 9e33
    tags:  # 9e33
    - smart_test  # 9e33
    - trunk_green  # 9e33
    - fast_smart_test_plan  # 9e33
    isbefore: []  # 9e33
    duplicate_of: []  # 9e33
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 9e33
    pr_ref:  # 9e33
    description: "FOUND 2026-11-18 ~01:00 UTC by the planted-fault campaign for the by-statement selector (210 faults, one statement each, in functions outside the selector's code; 104 ran tests; 93 failed at least one test; 653 failed tests). Notebook: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/fast_smart_test_build_11172026/bar/ (campaign_1_summary.txt, campaign_2_summary.txt, file_level_summary.txt, file_level.json).\n\nWHAT IS WRONG. The selection index pairs a test with a function when the test's own coverage context ran the function. Selection by block, and by statement even more, reads \"not paired\" as \"does not depend on it\". That is false wherever work is done ONCE and its result is shared:\n\n1. A MEMOISED FUNCTION. hypergumbo_core/linkers/view_template_laravel.py: `@lru_cache(maxsize=64) def _get_php_parser()`. The index pairs it with 3 of the 23 tests of test_view_template_laravel.py (whichever called it first in each worker). With its return replaced by `return None`, 9 other tests of that file failed: tests coverage can speak for, not paired with the function. 20 functions in the measured packages carry lru_cache / cache; hand-written caches were not counted.\n\n2. A FIXTURE SHARED BY A MODULE OR SESSION. packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_stable_id_floor_gate.py: the analysis runs once, in the setup of the first test (test_every_fixture_emits_a_symbol: 3,831 pairs, all from its setup phase, so it is now 'unmeasured'). With one assignment in entrypoints._take_dealing_ties replaced by None, test_every_fixture_node_id_names_its_own_kind and test_floor_census_matches_baseline failed: measured, and not paired with the function.\n\n3. A TEST THAT WORKS BOTH IN PROCESS AND THROUGH A SUBPROCESS. test_import_closure.py::test_the_helper_takes_a_function_the_index_saw_run_at_import (and its neighbour) build an index in process and then run coverage-select in a subprocess. With one call dropped from import_closure.ImportGraph.__init__ they failed: measured (their own call ran measured code), not paired with the function (the subprocess ran it).\n\nWHAT IT MEANS FOR THE BY-STATEMENT SELECTOR. Applied test by test, it would have left out 13 failing tests in 3 of the 93 runs. Its run record does not call these misses (failed_not_selected counts only tests that reached the changed function; all 13 reached it in no recorded way), and `coverage-select statement-bar` printed MET (88 runs by the merged definition; 40 by the stricter count of runs in which the selector left tests out, 781 tests left out, none failed). I count the 13 as a miss of a TEST-LEVEL switch: it stays off (owner ruling: one miss, it stays off and is filed).\n\nAPPLIED FILE BY FILE (a test file is kept if one of its tests is selected, or if it holds a test coverage cannot speak for, as narrowing already requires): 0 of the 93 runs lose a failing test file. Shapes 1 and 3 are held because another test of the same file ran the function; shape 2 because the file holds an unmeasured test. That is an observation over 93 runs, not a guarantee: a cache filled by a test in ANOTHER file, or a subprocess test file with no in-process path to the changed code, would not be held. Today's live narrowing is file by file and keeps every file that touched the changed FILE (a wider keep set), so it holds these three cases too; whether it has the cross-file shape was not observed either way.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED: how many functions have a result shared across test files; whether a merged change ever lost a failing test this way."  # 9e33
    fields:  # 9e33
      statement: "A test that depends on code is selected when that code changes, whether or not the test's own coverage context ran it: code whose result outlives the context that ran it (a cache, a shared fixture, a subprocess) is not narrowed on the strength of who ran it."  # 9e33
      root_cause: "The index's test-to-function pairs record execution under a test's own context, and selection reads their absence as independence. A memoised function runs under its first caller only; a module- or session-scoped fixture under the first test's setup; a subprocess under no context at all."  # 9e33
- op: discuss  # 75eb
  at: '2026-11-18T02:36:18Z'  # 75eb
  by: agent  # 75eb
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 75eb
  clock: 2  # 75eb
  nonce: 75eb  # 75eb
  message: "OWNER DIRECTION, 2026-11-18 ~02:40 UTC (in the session, after I recommended against building a switch and described a file-level variant): 'i'd prefer to just make the original idea work', i.e. by-statement selection applied test by test. THE PLAN I GAVE THE OWNER, in order; nothing of it is built or filed as items yet: (1) FIXTURES: record each module-, class- or session-scoped fixture under its own coverage context, and which tests used it; every test that uses the fixture inherits what it ran. Exact. 99 such fixtures in 78 test files. (2) CACHES: a changed function under a cache selects every test that ran one of its callers, from the call graph already stored in the index; hand-written caches through the function that writes the module-level state. Static analysis; it can miss an unusual idiom. 22 cache decorators and 30 'global' statements in 14 files of the measured source. (3) SUBPROCESS: the recorder notes which tests start a child process, and those are never narrowed away. 164 of 1,373 test files start one. I offered the alternative of measuring coverage inside child processes (more work, keeps more of the saving); the owner has not chosen it. (4) PROOF: re-run the planted-fault campaign weighted toward these three shapes and require zero failing tests the selector would leave out, counted over every failed test and not only those that reached the changed function. I told the owner this ends at campaign-clean, not proven, and that CI gains only if the manifest lists tests and not files."  # 75eb
- op: discuss  # c82a
  at: '2026-11-18T03:03:41Z'  # c82a
  by: agent  # c82a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c82a
  clock: 3  # c82a
  nonce: c82a  # c82a
  message: "Owner, 2026-11-18, on the subprocess shape: asked whether measuring coverage inside child processes is feasible; shown the measurement (it works on a toy project; x1.3 to x4 per child; a child with a scrubbed environment, python -S or SIGKILL leaves nothing and no error; at most 4.0 percent of graph-named test time to gain against at most 2.4 percent given up by the rule, 0.14 percent with gitleaks exempt), the owner answered: 'cheaper alternative then'. So: a test that starts a child able to run Python is never narrowed, gitleaks exempt by name; no child-process coverage. Steps filed, none built: fixtures WI-zimiv-doguz-zabad-pivof-hupas-dutuh-kimol-fadas; decorator caches WI-masad-tunos-zorij-vukok-golib-bilap-hupil-siduj; hand-written caches and lazy state on shared objects WI-supar-ruluh-bozob-sahor-noduv-nohit-mazob-vuroz; the child-process rule WI-sopig-jarit-hahap-tomab-kanam-katad-huzun-sobus; the campaign re-run that all four block WI-gojov-rohul-savug-mulap-rirur-diguh-havij-dosih (it blocks the switch, WI-pugol-sajov-fazah-romaj-lotag-hatuj-kogap-tovor); the per-test CI manifest WI-kunah-rahuv-batab-bakar-vikat-jogig-fadit-durun (after the switch). Found while preparing: the recorder plugin (scripts/pytest_plugins/pytest_source_reads.py) already records per test the shared fixtures it used and the processes it started; the fixture and child-process steps build on it."  # c82a
